"messages":[{"content":"Solve the problem step by step. Give your final answer in \\boxed{...}.\n\nFind the sum of all the rational terms in the expansion of\n\\[(\\sqrt{2} + \\sqrt[3]{3})^{12}.\\]","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"6364e41f4fc21957083edfc75cfc95384e3a03dd0171dc3d6c8af19ab06d63d1"},"seconds":45.64152669906616,"seed":266083435269092868,"stop":"eos","text":"To solve for the sum of all the rational terms in the expansion of \\((\\sqrt{2} + \\sqrt[3]{3})^{12}\\), we will use the Binomial Theorem. The Binomial Theorem states that:\n\n\\[\n(a + b)^n = \\sum_{k=0}^{n} \\binom{n}{k} a^{n-k} b^k\n\\]\n\nIn our case, \\(a = \\sqrt{2}\\), \\(b = \\sqrt[3]{3}\\), and \\(n = 12\\). The general term in the expansion is:\n\n\\[\n\\binom{12}{k} (\\sqrt{2})^{12-k} (\\sqrt[3]{3})^k = \\binom{12}{k} 2^{\\frac{12-k}{2}} 3^{\\frac{k}{3}}\n\\]\n\nFor a term to be rational, the exponents of \\(\\sqrt{2}\\) and \\(\\sqrt[3]{3}\\) must be integers. This means that \\(\\frac{12-k}{2}\\) and \\(\\frac{k}{3}\\) must both be integers. Let's analyze the conditions under which this happens.\n\n1. \\(\\frac{12-k}{2}\\) is an integer if and only if \\(12-k\\) is even, which means \\(k\\) must be even.\n2. \\(\\frac{k}{3}\\) is an integer if and only if \\(k\\) is a multiple of 3.\n\nTherefore, \\(k\\) must be a common multiple of 2 and 3, i.e., \\(k\\) must be a multiple of 6. The possible values of \\(k\\) are \\(0, 6, 12\\).\n\nNow, we will find the rational terms for each of these values of \\(k\\):\n\n1. For \\(k = 0\\):\n\\[\n\\binom{12}{0} 2^{\\frac{12-0}{2}} 3^{\\frac{0}{3}} = \\binom{12}{0} 2^6 3^0 = 1 \\cdot 64 \\cdot 1 = 64\n\\]\n\n2. For \\(k = 6\\):\n\\[\n\\binom{12}{6} 2^{\\frac{12-6}{2}} 3^{\\frac{6}{3}} = \\binom{12}{6} 2^3 3^2 = 924 \\cdot 8 \\cdot 9 = 924 \\cdot 72 = 66528\n\\]\n\n3. For \\(k = 12\\):\n\\[\n\\binom{12}{12} 2^{\\frac{12-12}{2}} 3^{\\frac{12}{3}} = \\binom{12}{12} 2^0 3^4 = 1 \\cdot 1 \\cdot 81 = 81\n\\]\n\nThe sum of all the rational terms is:\n\\[\n64 + 66528 + 81 = 66673\n\\]\n\nThus, the sum of all the rational terms in the expansion of \\((\\sqrt{2} + \\sqrt[3]{3})^{12}\\) is \\(\\boxed{66673}\\)."},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{66673}","correct":true,"format":"boxed","parseable":true}}
